{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/5/prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d8e07eb2\" lines=\"29-47\">\n<description>\nLook up query glyphs in a reference table and mark whether they form a line.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background with two full-width pink separator rows.\n- Above the first separator is a query row of 5x5 glyph cells.\n- Between separators is a reference table of the same 5x5 cells; a glyph is\n  identified by both its color and its 3x3 shape.\n\nRule:\n- Green-fill the background inside every table cell whose glyph exactly\n  appears in the query row.\n- If the matched table cells form one complete row or one complete column,\n  also green-fill the query band and the bottom verdict band.\n- Otherwise leave the query band unchanged and fill the bottom verdict band red.\n\nKey insight: The bottom color is not a count; it is the yes/no answer to\nwhether the queried glyphs align as a full row or column in the table.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\" lines=\"84-101\">\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"80a900e0\" lines=\"69-82\">\n<description>\nExtend diagonal rays outward from outer-border segments adjacent to diamond shapes on a checkerboard.\n\nInput structure:\n- Checkerboard bg (two most common colors).\n- Hollow diamond outlines (8-pixel) in a non-bg color (inner border).\n- Short diagonal line segments in another non-bg color adjacent to each diamond edge (outer border).\n\nRule:\n- For each outer-border segment, determine the outward direction (perpendicular to the segment, away from the diamond).\n- From each endpoint of the segment, cast a diagonal ray outward, painting over bg pixels with the segment's color until hitting a non-bg pixel or the grid edge.\n\nKey insight: Rays emit only from the two endpoints of each outer-border segment, not every pixel, and travel diagonally away from the diamond.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9ba4a9aa\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReturn the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.\n\nInput structure\n- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,\n  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.\n\nRule\n- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.\n- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.\n- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.\n\nKey insight\n- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide\n  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"78332cb0\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nReorder 5×5 blocks into a connected strip by matching edge foreground pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; sep = divider color (full row/column lines).\n- Several 5×5 sprite blocks in a grid, separated by sep lines.\n\nRule:\n- Two blocks \"connect\" when their facing edges share foreground pixels at the same offset (e.g., A's right edge ∩ B's left edge).\n- Chain all blocks in the orientation (horizontal or vertical) that admits a complete sequence.\n- Output is a 1×N strip of blocks in chain order, separated by sep lines.\n\nKey insight: Edge-contact overlap determines both block ordering and output orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3d6c6e23\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nMove each vertical stream of pixels into a bottom-aligned centered pile.\n\nInput structure\n- A mostly background grid contains one or more vertical source columns of\n  foreground pixels.\n- Each source column has n^2 foreground pixels, and the top-to-bottom color\n  sequence splits into monochrome groups of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...\n\nRule\n- Clear the canvas to background.\n- For each source column, read the foreground colors from top to bottom and\n  rebuild them at the bottom below the same column as odd-width centered\n  rows of lengths 1, 3, 5, ...\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered vertical pixels are falling grains; a square number of\n  grains decomposes into the odd row widths of a centered pyramid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"da6e95e5\" lines=\"70-81\">\n<description>\nExtract the central object from the marker cluster whose markers have the same color.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background and several clusters; each cluster has four\nsame-color corner marker objects around one central monochromatic object.\nRule\nFind every central object enclosed by four aligned corner markers of one color,\nthen choose the unique one whose object color equals the marker color.\nKey insight\nThe answer is not the largest or most frequent object; it is the center of the\nself-colored cluster, cropped to that object's bounding box with background kept.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"faa9f03d\" lines=\"36-52\">\n<description>\nRepair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.\n- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.\n- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.\n- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.\n- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.\n- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.\n\nKey insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"53fb4810\" lines=\"105-119\">\n<description>\nExtend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.\n- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.\n\nRule:\n- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.\n- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.\n- '+' pixels are never overwritten.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\" lines=\"74-88\">\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e84fef15\" lines=\"39-59\">\n<description>\nReconstruct the repeated tile from its separated copies and mark the missing pixels in the damaged copy blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a lattice of equal-size blocks separated by one-cell solid rows\n  and columns of the same separator color.\n- All blocks are copies of one motif except exactly one block, where some\n  motif pixels have been overwritten by the motif background color.\n- Blue is absent from the input and is reserved as the output marker color.\n\nRule:\n- Recover the motif by taking the unique majority color at each local block\n  coordinate across all block copies.\n- Find the single block that differs from this consensus motif.\n- Return the consensus motif, recoloring the differing local coordinates blue.\n\nKey insight:\n- The repeated copies provide redundancy: the damaged positions are exactly\n  the coordinates where the lone nonconforming copy has background instead of\n  the consensus motif color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"4df5b0ae\" lines=\"27-41\">\n<description>\nPack the colored sprites along the bottom edge from smallest to largest.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and several monochrome 4-connected\nforeground sprites; a full-grid one-cell frame may surround the scene.\n\nRule\nRemove any full-grid rectangular frame, sort the remaining sprites by pixel\narea from smallest to largest, and paste their bounding-box sprites left to\nright with each sprite bottom-aligned.\n\nKey insight\nThe original locations do not determine the final slots; each sprite's area\ngives the packing order, while its shape and color are preserved.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\nbottom layer to the top layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"15\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83b6b474\" lines=\"95-111\">\n<description>\nTranslate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.\n\nInput structure\nA grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic\n4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line\nsprites.\n\nRule\nPreserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and\nplace the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular\nframe exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count\ndetermines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame\nassembly with the least difference between height and width.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"16\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"17\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fd02da9e\" lines=\"26-41\">\n<description>\nReplace each colored corner marker with its corner-specific inward stencil in the same color.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is 8x8 with a single dominant background color.\n- Foreground pixels are marker pixels, each located at one of the four corners.\n- A marker's color is carried to the stencil selected by its corner position.\n\nRule\n- Erase each corner marker back to the background.\n- Top corner markers become an inward 2x2 block one cell away from the top and side edges.\n- Bottom corner markers become the mirrored three-pixel inward bent stencil shown by the bottom-corner examples.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker position selects the fixed stencil; the marker color only supplies the paint color, so multiple corners apply independently.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"18\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"34cfa167\" lines=\"94-117\">\n<description>\nComplete a four-corner framed rectangle from one decorated anchor rectangle and its diagonal mate.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Two same-color solid rectangular anchors of the same size sit on opposite\n  diagonal corners, with the first anchor above-left of the second.\n- The upper-left anchor has two one-cell-thick vertical control bars to its\n  right and two one-cell-thick horizontal control bars below it.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the anchor rectangle into the two missing corners of the implied\n  larger rectangle.\n- Repeat the right-side control bars horizontally between the left and right\n  anchors, using their spacing as the period, and draw the outer top and\n  bottom borders in the first right-control color.\n- Repeat the below-side control bars vertically between the top and bottom\n  anchors, using the same period, and draw the outer left and right borders\n  in the first below-control color.\n\nKey insight: The small control bars are the first two ticks of two periodic\nframe patterns; the diagonal anchor supplies the opposite corner and the\nmissing two anchor copies.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"19\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"25c199f5\" lines=\"30-44\">\n<description>\nPack the non-background rows from three separated panels into one panel, scanning panels right-to-left.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid contains three equal-size panels separated by two full-height divider columns.\n- The background is the dominant color; divider pixels use a color that appears only in the divider columns.\n\nRule:\n- Read panels from right to left, and within each panel read rows from top to bottom.\n- Keep each row that contains any non-background pixel, discarding all-background rows.\n- Place the kept rows at the bottom of a single panel-shaped output, padding above with background rows.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is row packing: the divider columns mark independent panels, and only the occupied rows are moved.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"20\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"142ca369\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nTrace diagonal rays from triangular arrows, reflecting off colored wall pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with monochrome 3-pixel L-shaped \"arrows\" (2x2 bbox missing one corner) and scattered colored wall pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Each arrow fires a ray diagonally away from its missing corner, painting bg cells in its color.\n- A head-on wall hit reverses both axes; a side-touch wall hit flips the touched axis only.\n- On any bounce the ray adopts the wall's color and recolors the contact cell.\n- Ray stops when it exits the grid or reaches another arrow.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct reflection types (corner vs side-touch) plus color inheritance at each bounce drive the path geometry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n你的任务：\n以参考题为标杆，运用发散性思维进行创新扩展。请发挥创造力，构思全新且巧妙的推理谜题。用中文创造全新的 ARC 谜题描述。\n创新方式包括但不限于：\n**机制重组**：将多道参考题各自的核心机制提取出来，组合成一个新的多步管道，用到新的场景。\n**机制变异**：取几道参考题的核心机制，以机制为基础进行创新并与其他多种机制进行创新重组，使解题路径发生质变而非量变。\n**逆向构造**：取一道参考题的输出模式，思考\"什么样的输入规则能产生类似的输出结构但需要完全不同的推理过程\"。\n\n严格使用下面的 XML 风格格式输出：\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n请一次性生成 5 个 `<new_puzzle>`，若有较多合适的创新，也可以生成更多，不设上限，但不要输出额外解释。\n\n注意事项：\n1. 任务不应依赖大量先验知识或复杂计算，而应依赖对模式、结构与空间关系的直觉推理。\n2. 关于\"计算\"的边界：\n   - **允许使用**（这些都是人类能心算的视觉量）：数像素 / 孔洞 / 对象 / 邻居（≤10 量级）、比大小、按某个可见属性排序、奇偶判断、取最大 / 最小、用一个计数值作为另一个等量几何量（如 N 个像素 → 长度 N 的射线、面积 N → 边长 N）、判断接触 / 相邻 / 包含、粗略中心位置（簇的视觉中点）、\"距离\"作为\"几步\"而不是公式等人类能够直观感受计算的内容。\n   - **不建议使用**（这些是算法量而非视觉量，会迫使解题者依赖公式而非视觉直觉）：曼哈顿 / 欧氏 / Chebyshev 距离作为精确查表键、凸包面积、二进制位编码 / 位运算、模运算 / 余数、拓扑距离（BFS / 最短路 / 跳数）、精确几何质心坐标、凸角 / 凹角计数等复杂且不易被看出来的计算。\n3. 问题必须能从少量示例中推断出来（通常为 3 对训练样例 + 1 个测试样例）。设计时从人类解题者的视觉角度思考：规则涉及的所有关键要素（对象、关系、属性）必须在网格上肉眼可见，不应依赖人眼无法直接观察到的隐含信息（例如颜色id是不可用的）或需要推算的隐藏属性。规则背后的逻辑本身可以有一定挑战性，但解题所需的所有线索必须能从网格中直接获取。\n4. 生成的问题描述旨在促进后续用于创建题目网格的代码生成。因此，对输入 / 输出网格的描述以及规则总结必须极其详细；语言风格应易于理解；对于特定对象（如箭头），必须在描述中明确其绘制方式。\n5. 描述谜题时避免使用 0 或 1 这类颜色编号；应使用颜色名称，例如 \"red\"。可用颜色如下：Black、Blue、Red、Green、Yellow、Grey、Magenta、Orange、Azure、Maroon。\n6. 5 个新题的核心推理机制应明显不同——即从观察样例到归纳规则的认知路径不同，而不仅仅是场景、对象或颜色不同。\n7. 题目难度应来自机制的隐蔽性而非步骤数。用参考题作为难度标尺——如果你生成的题一个样例就能猜到规则，说明机制太透明，需要加深隐蔽性。\n8. 优先从参考题的机制出发进行重组或推演，而非从物理隐喻出发。物理隐喻容易导致大量题目归结为一种模式，例如\"沿方向移动到阻挡处停止\"。\n9. 题目的难度优先来自规则的**结构层数**，而不是单一步骤的复杂度。关于\"结构层数\"的判定标准：**只有解题者需要主动推理才能完成的步骤才算一层**。肉眼一扫即可获得的信息（如\"找到缺口在哪一侧\"、\"数出像素个数\"、\"识别哪个对象最大\"）不构成独立的推理层——它们只是变换的前置输入，不增加认知难度。真正的多层结构是指每一层都需要解题者发现一个非显然的规律。以两层结构为例：\n   - ✗ 伪两层：\"找到缺口方向\"→\"把东西推到对面\" — 第一步肉眼可见，实际只有一层推理。\n   - ✓ 真两层：\"通过孔洞数量匹配颜色图例\"→\"用匹配到的颜色重绘对象\" — 两步都需要非平凡的归纳。\n\n   如果你发现自己写出的题只是\"对每个对象，根据它的某个属性做某变换\"的单步规则，请引入一个需要主动推理的前置步骤。**用堆叠 tie-break 规则或复杂数学公式来\"加难度\"是劣解；用多一层需要归纳的结构嵌套来\"加难度\"才是好解**。\n10. 在符合ARC题目和提示词要求的基础上，鼓励尝试创新规则为多层结构的题目，不局限于两层，可以创新三层或四层结构的题目，但不可为了多结构层数违背要求。\n\n生成后对生成的题执行以下四项审查（审查内容不需要保存到文件）：\n1. 原创性\n  新题不可与上述参考题相同或类似，不可只是替换颜色、方向、对象类型等表层要素。判断标准：新题的核心推理路径应与任何一道参考题不同。\n2. 确定性\n  规则完全确定，同一输入只能产生唯一输出，每一步操作都有明确定义。\n3. 可实现性\n  所有必要细节都已给出——对象如何生成、如何识别、如何变换、边界情况如何处理，读完描述后可以直接写代码。\n4. ARC 合规性\n  网格尺寸合理（≤30×30），使用颜色不超过 10 种，规则复杂度适中，人类能从 3-5 个样例中发现规律。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
